	       iinntt ffppoorrtt;;	   //** FFaarr ((rreemmoottee)) ppoorrtt **//
	       cchhaarr **iiddeennttiiffiieerr;;   //** NNoorrmmaallllyy uusseerr nnaammee **//
	       cchhaarr **ooppssyyss;;	   //** OOSS **//
	       cchhaarr **cchhaarrsseett;;	   //** CChhaarrsseett ((wwhhaatt ddiidd yyoouu eexxppeecctt??)) **//
	  }} IIDDEENNTT;;

     For a full description of the different fields, refer to
     _R_F_C-_1_4_1_3.

     All data returned by iiddeenntt__llooookkuupp (including the IDENT
     struct) points to malloc'd data, which can be freed with a
     call to iiddeenntt__ffrreeee.. iiddeenntt__llooookkuupp returns 0 on error or
     timeout. Presently, this should normally be taken to mean
     that the remote site is not running an IDENT server, but it
     might naturally be caused by other network related problems
     as well.  NNoottee tthhaatt all fields of the IDENT struct need not
     necessarily be set.

     iiddeenntt__iidd takes the same parameters as iiddeenntt__llooookkuupp but only
     returns a pointer to a malloc'd area containing the _i_d_e_n_t_i_f_-
     _i_e_r string, which is probably the most wanted data from the
     IDENT query.

     iiddeenntt__ffrreeee frees all data areas associated with the IDENT
     struct pointed to by _i_d, including the struct itself.

			 _L_o_w-_l_e_v_e_l _c_a_l_l_s

     The low-level calls can be used when greater flexibility is
     needed. For example, if non-blocking I/O is needed, or mul-
     tiple queries to the same host are to be made.

     iidd__ooppeenn opens a connection to the remote IDENT server
     referred to by _f_a_d_d_r. The timeout is specified by _t_i_m_e_o_u_t. A
     null-pointer means wait indefinitely, while a pointer to a
     zero-valued _t_i_m_e_v_a_l struct sets non-blocking I/O, in the
     same way as for sseelleecctt((22)).. iidd__ooppeenn returns a pointer to an
     iidd__tt datum, which is an opaque structure to be used as
     future reference to the opened connection. When using non-
     blocking I/O it might however be useful to access the under-
     lying socket file descriptior, which can be gotten at
     through the iidd__ffiilleennoo macro described below.

     iidd__cclloossee closes the connection opened with iidd__ooppeenn and frees
     all data associated with _i_d.

     iidd__qquueerryy sends off a query to a remote IDENT server.  _l_p_o_r_t
     and _f_p_o_r_t are sent to the server to identify the connection
     for which identification is needed.  _t_i_m_e_o_u_t is given as for
     iidd__ooppeenn.. If successful, iidd__qquueerryy returns the number of bytes
     sent to the remote server. If not, -1 is returned and eerrrrnnoo
     is set.



